From: Gleb Pesin <dormancygrace@gmail.com>
Subject: The SCTP packets of one usrsctp call leave in batched sends

usrsctp hands each packet to the write callback on its own, and each became one DTLS record and one sendto: a data
channel at full rate spent a fifth of its CPU in those system calls. While a BurstScope is open on a thread,
DtlsTransport collects its records there, and the outermost scope sends them through juice_send_batch (UDP_SEGMENT),
32 at a time. SctpTransport opens a scope around each call into usrsctp that sends: a send, a flush, and the input of
a received packet (a SACK lets the next DATA out); DtlsTransport opens one over the records it drains in one wake,
so the DATA that several SACKs let out leaves together. usrsctp's own timer thread opens none, so a retransmission
or a heartbeat still leaves at once.

Nothing is sent before the scope ends, and every scope is opened before its lock is taken: the send, and its wait
for a full socket, run after the SCTP and DTLS locks are released. An open scope takes every record, however many:
there is no count past which a record is sent at once, under those locks and ahead of the ones taken before it.

A record goes to the end of its transport's queue when it is made, so the queue has the records of all threads in
the order they were made. When a scope ends, what the queue holds may leave, and one thread at a time sends it from
the front: the records leave in that order whichever threads made them, and a thread that finds another one sending
leaves its records to it. A record made on a thread without a scope (usrsctp's timer thread) leaves at once only when
nothing of the transport is queued or being sent, and holds the sender's place while it does; otherwise it goes
behind the queued records and leaves with them.

The layer below tells how many records the socket took (sendBatchPrefix). The SCTP stack was already told that all
of them were sent, so the ones not taken are kept and given again, without the ones taken. The first shortfall sets
one time, a second later: every later call is made only before it and may wait for the socket only for what is left
of it (juice_send_batch_within), so the batch cannot run past it. Records queued behind a batch that waits get no
second of their own: after that time they are offered once without a wait. A transport that cannot send any more
ends its batch at once. What is given up is counted once in UnsentMediaDatagrams and logged.

Through a TURN relay a batch goes out one channel message at a time. The same bound holds there: every socket wait
of those sends, the channel's own requests included, ends by the caller's time, and no datagram is begun after it.

DtlsTransport reads at most 32 queued datagrams in one receive run and leaves the rest to the next run, so a queue
that is filled as fast as it is drained cannot hold back what the input let out: each run's records leave when it
returns, after its lock.
